# A custom installCommand (tried first) disables Vercel's automatic function
# bundle optimization entirely — that dropped the effective cap from the
# documented 500MB down to ~225MB and a plain build blew past it (315.92MB).
# So: no installCommand (see vercel.json — Vercel auto-detects and installs
# requirements.txt with its own optimizer), pyproject.toml hidden below so
# requirements.txt wins detection instead of pyproject's incomplete base
# deps. dup_index.joblib now ships (14MB, needed for the "possibly related"
# comment section) — the last measured pre-optimization bundle was 281MB
# with real headroom to the 500MB cap, so this fits comfortably.
# effort.joblib (21MB) stays cut; load_effort_predictor() already returns
# None on a missing file so that feature just degrades gracefully (Docker/
# Fly deploys are unaffected, still ship all heads).

.git
.github
.claude
.venv
venv
__pycache__
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
_archive

# A separate, unrelated project (its own git repo, a pnpm monorepo with a
# huge node_modules) that happens to live nested inside this directory on
# disk. Sweeping it into this Python backend's upload blew past Vercel's
# 15000-file limit.
Elegant-Interface-Design/

# Secrets — never in a deployment bundle, even gitignored ones.
.env
*.pem

# Research data / notebooks / historical run reports — not needed to serve
# predictions; the ledger goes to Postgres on this deploy (DATABASE_URL).
data/
reports/
notebook/
scripts/

# Docs, tests, project meta.
docs/
tests/
conftest.py
BUILD_PLAN_2.md
PROGRESS.md
README.md
PRIVACY.md
SUPPORT.md
LICENSE

# Model artifacts: champion.joblib (required), category.joblib (676KB),
# and dup_index.joblib (14MB, powers "possibly related" + assignment
# suggestions) ship on Vercel. rf_balanced.joblib is settings.py's fallback
# for when champion.joblib is missing, moot here since champion always
# ships. effort.joblib (21MB) stays cut for bundle size — see note above.
models/*.md
models/rf.joblib
models/rf_balanced.joblib
models/logreg.joblib
models/logreg_balanced.joblib
models/effort.joblib

# Local-dev / Docker-only files. pyproject.toml IS excluded here — Vercel
# prefers pyproject.toml over requirements.txt for auto-detection when both
# are present, and pyproject's base deps deliberately exclude the service
# extras. requirements.txt alone is enough for Vercel's Python-project
# detection to succeed.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
pyproject.toml
